## Inheritance Tax Summary

We each have a Nil Rate Band (NRB) of £325,000 and, if your property is passed to direct descendants, a Main Residence Nil Rate Band (RNRB) of £175,000. Any unused NRB can be transferred to a surviving spouse or civil partner, meaning up to £1 million can be passed on tax-free on second death.

Historically, pensions have not formed part of your estate for IHT purposes, making them a valuable estate planning tool. However, legislation has now been passed to change this.

From 6 April 2027, most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits (such as drawdown funds and uncrystallised pots) will be included in your taxable estate for IHT. Exceptions include pensions paid to a surviving spouse or civil partner, dependents' scheme pensions, and death-in-service benefits. Executors will be responsible for declaring pension values as part of the IHT return and may use new options for settling any tax due directly from pension funds.

Currently, your Inheritance Tax liability will be £0. We will continue to monitor your IHT position at our future reviews.

## Your Objectives

We reviewed your needs, objectives and time horizons and agreed the following:

-  You want to ensure you can continue to afford your lifestyle through your lifetime.
-  You want to retire in 2 years time with a household income of £1,700 net per month.
-  You would like a clearer understanding of your pension arrangements and to ensure they are appropriately structured for your retirement. You also prefer a more streamlined approach to managing your pensions, rather than maintaining five separate plans with different providers.

<!-- page: 4 -->

## Attitude to Risk &amp; Capacity for loss

## Your attitude to risk

You completed an Attitude to Risk questionnaire. We felt that this didn't fully reflect your risk appetite. We agreed that you are a risk rating 5 rather than a 4 as per the questionnaire. You are happy to proceed with a Risk rating of 5.

You confirmed that you wish to increase your Risk rating to 5- Balanced investor, you are aware that this is higher than your agreed risk rating and are happy to take on this risk with your pension. You have confirmed that you are not reliant upon your pension funds and wish for them to have the best growth potential over a 10-year period and feel a balanced approach to this investment would achieve this goal. You are aware that your funds may go up as well as down, and the higher risk you take with your funds, potential there is for larger drops in value.

As a Balanced investor, you do not see yourself as a particularly cautious person and have no strong positive or negative associations with the notion of taking risk.  If you have some experience in investments and a degree of understanding of financial matters, then you may be suited to a Balanced approach to investing.  You may also be suited to this approach if you make investment decisions reasonably quickly and don't tend to be particularly anxious about those decisions.  As a Balanced investor, you can be inclined to look for a combination of investments with differing levels of risk and understand that you may need to take some risk to meet your investment goals.

It is important to continually review your risk profile moving forward to ensure that this level of risk remains in line with your personal attitudes, and as such I will keep this under review in line with our ongoing review meetings.

## Capacity for loss

John, you are aware that investment values can rise as well as fall, and although your experience is limited to your workplace pension arrangements, you understand that returns are not guaranteed. You confirmed that any potential underperformance would not have a detrimental impact on your day-to-day living. You are in receipt of a full State Pension of approximately £230 per week, continue to work as an HGV driver earning around £300 per week net, and receive an additional £20 per week from your role with the Sea Cadets. Kim contributes a further £1,100 per month from her employment and will also receive a full State Pension of around £230 per week at State Pension age. Your combined income comfortably meets your household expenditure of approximately £1,700 per month, you hold savings of around £6,000, and have no outstanding liabilities. You also hold pension funds totalling approximately £106,000, which form the basis of your retirement provision. We therefore agreed that you have a medium capacity for loss, consistent with your agreed Attitude to Risk level of 5, and appropriate to your current aims and objectives.

