## My Recommendation

Following your request, we have now completed a full assessment of the options available to you.  My recommendation accounts for several important factors including your answers to our Attitude to Risk Questionnaire and an analysis of the available options.

When carrying out this review I conducted a full review of your existing pension scheme in relation to the charges, investment performance, accessible fund range, and retirement options available. This helps to understand the competitive nature of each pension scheme. Once this information was analysed, I then compared each pension scheme against the most suitable alternative option from the following:

-  Option 1 - Leave the existing pension scheme untouched
-  Option 2 - Leave the existing pension scheme in place and switch investment funds
-  Option 3 - Switch the existing pension scheme into another existing pension arrangement
-  Option 4 - Switch your existing schemes into your employer's pension arrangement
-  Option 5 - Set up a new pension plan to move your existing pension schemes into
-  This is your current workplace scheme, and you and your employer are still contributing to this and will continue to until your retirement.
-  If you were to transfer away, you would miss out on the valuable benefit of your employer's contributions.
-  I do not recommend that you consolidate into this plan as the funds do not meet your risk profile, and the plan itself is 0.06% more expensive than the recommended solution. Plus, they have a £4.50 annual admin charge.
-  There are alternative funds available within the plan; however, there are no Multi-Asset funds and only a limited range of other options. Your current fund has performed well and is delivering a strong return. You should consider switching your investments to better align with your risk rating by moving into the Global Investments (up to 60% shares) fund. This fund has not performed as strongly as your current selection, but is less volatile and aligns with your risk appetite.
-  I do not recommend that you consolidate into this plan as the funds do not meet your risk profile; Currently, Scottish Widows does not offer a single multi-asset fund that aligns with your risk rating of 5. To achieve this level of risk, we would need to combine two funds. One of which is entirely invested in money market (cash) holdings. This would effectively leave around 20-30% of your portfolio out of the market. Our goal is to ensure that your entire investment is working for you, so we want to find a solution that keeps 100% of your funds invested in the market.
-  I also do not recommend you invest in this plan as it does not allow adviser management, which you would like to benefit from to ensure you are on the right track for retirement.
-  If you were to transfer all your pensions into this plan, it would limit how you can withdraw funds. In the future, this would be important to you, as you would be unable to access this pension flexibly.
-  I would recommend you withdraw the full amount of this pension under the small pots rules and put the money into your savings account. This would not detract from your financial retirement plan. Under the small pot rules, a £200 pension fund withdrawal would be taxed with 25% taken as tax-free cash and the remaining 75% taxed as income at your marginal tax rate. We estimate you would receive £170 net. If you were to leave this pot to grow until you retire, you could potentially take the full amount within your personal allowance, meaning no tax would be due.

